=== DEFECT FOUND AND FIXED ===

BandCriterion.contains() ANSWERED FALSE FOR VALUES GENUINELY IN THE BAND.
S-1 prints its ranges at two decimal places (0.00-0.19, 0.20-0.39, ...), which
leaves unprinted gaps: 0.195 is in no printed range, yet it is plainly
NEGLIGIBLE. contains() tested against the printed literals, so it returned
False for 0.195, 0.395, 0.599 and 0.799 -- a trap for any caller that used it
to route an assertion to a criterion.

Assessment itself was never wrong: assess_assertion() resolves the band through
ConfidenceBand.for_value() (T01.5.1), which is authoritative. But a public
predicate that disagrees with the authoritative boundary is latent
miscalibration of exactly the kind S-1 exists to prevent.

Fixed by making contains() delegate to the authoritative band while retaining
low/high as the S-1 text for display. The two are verified to agree at every
printed gridpoint by an import-time guard, and mutation M29 confirms the
delegation is load-bearing.

=== SPECIFICATION NOTES ===
No document conflicts found. S-1, R-3, N-3, B-05, IOM 2.3 and the backlog agree
on every binding point. S-1's printed ranges match the already-implemented
ConfidenceBand boundaries exactly (verified before any code was written).

SPECIFICATION GAP SURFACED, NOT FILLED.
S-1 gives each band a test ("Are there >=2 equally good alternative
conclusions?") but the object model has NO ATTRIBUTE in which an engine records
its answer. Adding one would change the object model, which this task may not
do. The conformance layer therefore accepts a declared alternative count as an
OPTIONAL input and reports UNASSESSED when it is absent -- never CONFORMANT.
The absence of a ratified field is a real gap and is recorded here rather than
papered over.

DELIBERATELY NOT IMPLEMENTED:
  - Any alternative-counting algorithm. S-1 makes counting OBSERVABLE, not
    COMPUTABLE: it requires judging which readings of the inputs are credible,
    which is engine judgement over object content. Computing it would fabricate
    the rubric.
  - Any statistical or empirical calibration. That is N-3's measure O2, which
    needs realised outcomes that do not exist before P8, and is scheduled at
    T08.3.5. B-05 is explicit that the rubric governs now and empiricism
    corrects later.
  - Counts for NEGLIGIBLE and STRONG. S-1 defines those two bands
    qualitatively (reviewer disagreement; assumption-dependence), so a
    count-based check against them returns UNASSESSED. Attributing a number
    would invent a threshold nobody ratified and would manufacture deviations.
  - Any acceptance-rule change. No ratified source makes calibration a
    validation rule; V1-V12 do not include it. AC2 says deviations are
    RECORDED, and recording is all that happens. The rule count is still 68.
  - Any change to evidential_support or effective_confidence. S-1's own Known
    Tensions restrict the rubric to assertion_confidence (M-59 governs the
    other).
